---
import SimLayout from '../../../layouts/SimLayout.astro';
---

<SimLayout
  areaSlug="physics"
  title="Nuclear Fission"
  description="One neutron splits one nucleus, and two or three more fly out. Each of those either splits the next nucleus, gets swallowed, or leaks out the side. Whether that count lands above or below one is the whole difference between a lump of metal, a power station, and a bomb."
>
  <div class="sim">
    <div class="sim-stage">
      <canvas id="fis" class="fis-canvas" width="1000" height="1040"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="sim-panel">
      <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap">
        <button class="btn" id="run">Pause</button>
        <button class="btn ghost" id="reset">Reset</button>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Scenario</label>
        <div class="seg" id="scenario">
          <button data-s="lump" class="active">Inert lump</button>
          <button data-s="reactor">Power reactor</button>
          <button data-s="chernobyl">Chernobyl</button>
          <button data-s="bomb">Hiroshima</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Time rate <b id="rateval">0.30×</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="rate" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="39" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>watch one neutron</span><span>watch the chain</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Enrichment (% U-235) <b id="enrval">0.7%</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="enr" min="0.7" max="93" step="0.1" value="0.7" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>natural</span><span>weapons</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Core radius <b id="szval">15.6 cm</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="size" min="20" max="900" step="2" value="156" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>2 cm</span><span>90 cm</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Neutron reflector <b id="refval">0%</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="refl" min="0" max="95" step="1" value="0" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>bare</span><span>tamper</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="control">
        <label>Control rods <b id="rodval">0%</b></label>
        <input type="range" id="rod" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="0" />
        <div class="scale-hint"><span>out</span><span>in</span></div>
      </div>

      <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="autoctl" /> Auto control (hold&nbsp;k&nbsp;≈&nbsp;1)</label>
      <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" id="src" checked /> Background neutron source</label>

      <div class="readout">
        <div class="row"><span id="l1">Multiplication k</span><b id="r1">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l2">Generation</span><b id="r2">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l3">Free neutrons</span><b id="r3">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l4">Fissions</span><b id="r4">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l5">Core mass (sphere)</span><b id="r5">—</b></div>
        <div class="row"><span id="l6">State</span><b id="r6">—</b></div>
      </div>

      <p style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--text-faint);margin:0;line-height:1.5">
        Every neutron on screen really walks until it splits a nucleus, is
        captured, or escapes. k is counted from those events, not assumed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="briefing">
    <span class="briefing-tag">The scene</span>
    <h3 id="brief-title">A lump of natural uranium</h3>
    <p id="brief-body"></p>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="observe">
    <li>
      Turn the <b>time rate</b> right down and follow one neutron. It flies, it
      hits a nucleus, and one of three things happens. Orange means it split the
      nucleus and two or three new neutrons burst out. Grey means a nucleus
      swallowed it. Blue at the rim means it leaked away. <b>That is the entire
      simulation.</b> k is just the score: how many new neutrons each departing
      one leaves behind. Every scenario loads at its own sensible speed, and you
      can always slow it down further to watch, or speed it up to see the chain.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Inert lump</b> is natural uranium, 0.7% U-235. Almost every neutron is
      captured or leaks before it finds a nucleus worth splitting. k stays near
      zero and nothing catches. This is why uranium ore is safe to hold.
    </li>
    <li>
      Raise <b>enrichment</b>, then raise the <b>core radius</b>. Enrichment
      gives a neutron more targets. Size gives it less edge to escape through: a
      bigger core leaks proportionally less. Either one pushes k up, and where k
      reaches one you have the <b>critical mass</b>. With weapons-grade metal and
      no reflector that happens at about <b>52 kg</b>, the real figure.
    </li>
    <li>
      Add a <b>reflector</b>. It bounces escaping neutrons back in, so a smaller
      and lighter core goes critical. Push in the <b>control rods</b> and they
      eat neutrons instead, pulling k back down. Those two sliders are the brake
      and the accelerator of every reactor on earth.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Press Chernobyl</b> and read the card under the simulator: that is the
      state Reactor 4 was actually left in at 01:23 on 26 April 1986. Then walk
      into the accident yourself, because it is only four moves.
      <br /><br />
      <b>1.</b> Load <b>Power reactor</b> first, and let it settle. Rods a third
      in, auto control on, k locked on 1.000, population graph flat. This is a
      station making electricity.
      <br />
      <b>2.</b> Uncheck <b>Auto control</b>. Nothing corrects you now. Nothing
      changes yet either, which is the trap: the core looks exactly as safe.
      <br />
      <b>3.</b> Drag <b>Control rods</b> down toward 0, the way the operators
      did to fight the xenon. k lifts off one, to about <b>1.14</b>. Only 14%
      per generation, and it looks like nothing.
      <br />
      <b>4.</b> Watch. The graph bends, then goes vertical, and the core floods
      with neutrons. 14% compounded over forty generations multiplies the power
      nearly two hundred times, and the generations take a heartbeat each.
      <br />
      <b>5.</b> Try to save it: shove the rods back in. Early and k drops under
      one and the core settles. A few seconds late and there is nothing left to
      do, which is exactly the position the operators found themselves in.
      <br /><br />
      Now notice what the panel refuses to give you: <b>no nuclear yield</b>.
      Reactor fuel is far too dilute to detonate. It boiled, burst its pipes and
      set its graphite alight, which was catastrophe enough.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Hiroshima</b> is the other side of the line: weapons-grade metal,
      compact, reflected, nothing absorbing. k leaps to about two, so the
      population <b>doubles every generation</b> instead of creeping. The graph
      runs off the top, the core detonates, and the yield strip lands on 15
      kilotons from 64 kg of uranium, which is what the real bomb held.
    </li>
    <li>
      Four sliders moved between a paperweight, a power station and a weapon. The
      physics never changed. Only k did.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</SimLayout>

<script>
  import { setupCanvas, makeText, subHtml } from '../../../lib/sim';
  import { equationBar } from '../../../lib/equation';

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // A chain reaction, counted rather than assumed. Neutrons walk through a disk
  // of uranium; on each interaction they split a nucleus (handing back 2-3 fresh
  // neutrons), get captured, or scatter, and at the rim they leak away or are
  // reflected. k on screen is literally (neutrons born) / (neutrons ended) over
  // a short window: nothing forces it to any value, it falls out of the geometry
  // and the cross sections.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  const NU = 2.43; // mean neutrons released per U-235 fission
  const E_FISSION = 3.204e-11; // J per fission (~200 MeV)
  const RHO_U = 19.1; // g/cm³, uranium metal
  const SPEED = 46; // cm per sim-second the neutrons travel
  const MAXN = 3200; // neutron cap; past this the chain is "diverging"
  // The clock is normalised so a neutron takes CROSS_SECONDS (at rate 1) to
  // travel one core radius, whatever the fuel or the zoom. Pacing by generations
  // instead would be useless to watch: in dilute fuel a neutron scatters its way
  // across the core a dozen times before anything absorbs it, so one generation
  // is a blur. This way the dots always move at a followable speed on screen.
  const CROSS_SECONDS = 4;
  const SRC_PER_GEN = 12; // background source strength, per generation

  type Scenario = 'lump' | 'reactor' | 'chernobyl' | 'bomb';

  const state = {
    enr: 0.007,
    R: 15.6, // core radius, cm
    refl: 0,
    rod: 0,
    rate: 0.25,
    running: true,
    auto: false,
    source: true,
    scenario: 'lump' as Scenario,
  };

  // The absolute scale is set so a bare sphere of 90% U-235 goes critical at
  // R = 8.7 cm, the real 52 kg bare critical mass. Multiplying every cross
  // section by a constant only rescales lengths, never any k, so this pins the
  // masses to reality without disturbing the ladder of scenarios.
  function sigmas(enr: number, rod: number) {
    const Sf = 0.0809 * enr; // fission, scales with how much U-235 is present
    const Sc = 0.00289 + 0.000462 * (1 - enr) + 0.00347 * rod; // U-238 + rods
    const Ss = 0.1734; // elastic scatter: redirects, never removes
    return { Sf, Sc, Ss, St: Sf + Sc + Ss, Sa: Sf + Sc };
  }
  /** Sim-seconds for one generation: how long a neutron lives. */
  const genTime = () => 1 / (sigmas(state.enr, state.rod).Sa * SPEED);
  const kInfinite = () => {
    const s = sigmas(state.enr, state.rod);
    return (NU * s.Sf) / s.Sa;
  };
  const coreMass = () => ((4 / 3) * Math.PI * state.R ** 3 * RHO_U) / 1000;

  /**
   * k without waiting for a chain: walk a few hundred throwaway neutrons through
   * exactly the same transport and count how many end in a fission.
   * k = ν · (fissions / histories). This lets the panel say "this assembly is
   * supercritical" the instant you move a slider.
   */
  function estimateK(samples = 1500): number {
    const s = sigmas(state.enr, state.rod);
    const R = state.R;
    const ds = 0.4; // interaction is memoryless, so any step is exact
    const pInt = 1 - Math.exp(-s.St * ds);
    let fis = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < samples; i++) {
      const r0 = R * Math.sqrt(Math.random());
      const a0 = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
      let x = Math.cos(a0) * r0, y = Math.sin(a0) * r0;
      const ang = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
      let vx = Math.cos(ang), vy = Math.sin(ang);
      for (let guard = 0; guard < 12000; guard++) {
        x += vx * ds;
        y += vy * ds;
        const rad = Math.hypot(x, y);
        if (rad > R) {
          if (Math.random() < state.refl) {
            const nx = x / rad, ny = y / rad;
            const dot = vx * nx + vy * ny;
            vx -= 2 * dot * nx;
            vy -= 2 * dot * ny;
            const over = rad - R;
            x -= nx * over * 2;
            y -= ny * over * 2;
            continue;
          }
          break; // leaked
        }
        if (Math.random() < pInt) {
          const roll = Math.random() * s.St;
          if (roll < s.Sf) { fis++; break; }
          if (roll < s.Sf + s.Sc) break;
          const na = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
          vx = Math.cos(na);
          vy = Math.sin(na);
        }
      }
    }
    return NU * (fis / samples);
  }

  /** The k to show: measured once a chain is running, predicted before that. */
  const kNow = () => (hasMeasure ? kEMA : kPred);

  // Re-estimating on every input event of a dragged slider is both wasteful and
  // jittery, so throttle it and smooth the result. A Monte Carlo answer carries
  // real noise, which is also why the predicted k is shown to two decimals and
  // the counted one to three.
  let lastEst = 0;
  function refreshK(force = false) {
    const now = performance.now();
    if (!force && now - lastEst < 70) return;
    lastEst = now;
    const k = estimateK();
    kPred = force || kPred === 0 ? k : kPred * 0.6 + k * 0.4;
  }

  type N = { x: number; y: number; vx: number; vy: number; age: number };
  let neutrons: N[] = [];
  // one visual mark per event, so the three fates are legible in slow motion
  type Ev = { x: number; y: number; life: number; kind: 'fission' | 'capture' | 'escape' };
  let events: Ev[] = [];
  type Frag = { x: number; y: number; vx: number; vy: number; life: number };
  let frags: Frag[] = [];

  let fissions = 0;
  let bornW = 0, endedW = 0;
  let kEMA = 0;
  let hasMeasure = false;
  let kPred = 0;
  let seedCount = 12;
  let gens = 0; // generations elapsed
  let srcClock = 0;
  let windowClock = 0;
  let nHist: number[] = [];
  let diverged = false;
  let detonated = false;
  let blastT = 0;

  function spawn(x: number, y: number) {
    // bornW counts only fission offspring (added in step), never the source
    if (neutrons.length >= MAXN) {
      diverged = true;
      return;
    }
    const a = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
    neutrons.push({ x, y, vx: Math.cos(a) * SPEED, vy: Math.sin(a) * SPEED, age: 0 });
  }

  function mark(x: number, y: number, kind: Ev['kind']) {
    // subsample once the core is crowded, or this floods at 3000 neutrons
    if (neutrons.length > 60 && Math.random() > 60 / neutrons.length) return;
    events.push({ x, y, life: 1, kind });
    if (events.length > 240) events.shift();
  }

  function build() {
    neutrons = [];
    events = [];
    frags = [];
    fissions = 0;
    bornW = 0;
    endedW = 0;
    kEMA = 0;
    hasMeasure = false;
    kPred = 0;
    refreshK(true);
    gens = 0;
    srcClock = 0;
    windowClock = 0;
    nHist = [];
    diverged = false;
    detonated = false;
    blastT = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < seedCount; i++) {
      const r = Math.random() * state.R * 0.6;
      const a = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
      spawn(Math.cos(a) * r, Math.sin(a) * r);
    }
  }

  function step(dt: number) {
    const s = sigmas(state.enr, state.rod);
    const ds = SPEED * dt;
    const pInteract = 1 - Math.exp(-s.St * ds);
    const R = state.R;

    if (state.source && !detonated) {
      srcClock += dt;
      // tied to the generation, so the source is equally strong relative to the
      // chain in every scenario rather than 20× stronger in reactor fuel
      const period = genTime() / SRC_PER_GEN;
      while (srcClock > period) {
        srcClock -= period;
        const r = Math.random() * R * 0.9;
        const a = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
        spawn(Math.cos(a) * r, Math.sin(a) * r);
      }
    }

    const survivors: N[] = [];
    for (const p of neutrons) {
      p.x += p.vx * dt;
      p.y += p.vy * dt;
      p.age += dt;
      const rad = Math.hypot(p.x, p.y);

      if (rad > R) {
        if (Math.random() < state.refl) {
          const nx = p.x / rad, ny = p.y / rad;
          const dot = p.vx * nx + p.vy * ny;
          p.vx -= 2 * dot * nx;
          p.vy -= 2 * dot * ny;
          const over = rad - R;
          p.x -= nx * over * 2;
          p.y -= ny * over * 2;
          survivors.push(p);
        } else {
          endedW++;
          mark(p.x, p.y, 'escape');
        }
        continue;
      }

      if (Math.random() < pInteract) {
        const roll = Math.random() * s.St;
        if (roll < s.Sf) {
          fissions++;
          endedW++;
          mark(p.x, p.y, 'fission');
          // the two fission fragments, purely so the split reads as a split
          const fa = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
          for (const sgn of [1, -1]) {
            frags.push({
              x: p.x, y: p.y,
              vx: Math.cos(fa) * sgn * state.R * 0.55,
              vy: Math.sin(fa) * sgn * state.R * 0.55,
              life: 1,
            });
          }
          if (frags.length > 120) frags.splice(0, frags.length - 120);
          const nu = NU + (Math.random() - 0.5) * 1.4;
          const count = Math.max(0, Math.floor(nu) + (Math.random() < nu % 1 ? 1 : 0));
          bornW += count;
          for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) spawn(p.x, p.y);
        } else if (roll < s.Sf + s.Sc) {
          endedW++;
          mark(p.x, p.y, 'capture');
        } else {
          const a = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
          p.vx = Math.cos(a) * SPEED;
          p.vy = Math.sin(a) * SPEED;
          survivors.push(p);
        }
      } else {
        survivors.push(p);
      }
    }
    neutrons = survivors;

    windowClock += dt;
    if (windowClock > genTime()) {
      if (endedW > 4) {
        const k = bornW / endedW;
        kEMA = hasMeasure ? kEMA * 0.8 + k * 0.2 : k;
        hasMeasure = true;
      }
      bornW = 0;
      endedW = 0;
      windowClock = 0;
    }

    // the control rods, working the way a real reactor's do
    if (state.auto && !detonated && hasMeasure) {
      const err = kEMA - 1.0;
      state.rod = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, state.rod + err * (dt / genTime()) * 0.35));
      (document.getElementById('rod') as HTMLInputElement).value = String(Math.round(state.rod * 100));
      (document.getElementById('rodval') as HTMLElement).textContent = `${Math.round(state.rod * 100)}%`;
    }

    gens += dt / genTime();

    if (neutrons.length >= MAXN && kEMA > 1.05) {
      diverged = true;
      if (kEMA > 1.55 && !detonated) detonated = true;
    }
  }

  // --- classification --------------------------------------------------------

  function regime(): { name: string; colour: string; note: string } {
    const k = kNow();
    if (detonated)
      return { name: 'detonation', colour: BAD, note: 'the whole core reacts at once' };
    if (k > 1.5)
      return { name: 'prompt supercritical', colour: BAD, note: 'doubling every generation: a weapon' };
    if (k > 1.02)
      return {
        name: 'supercritical',
        colour: WARN,
        note: diverged ? 'climbing faster than anyone can answer' : 'growing every generation',
      };
    if (k > 0.995)
      return { name: 'critical', colour: GOOD, note: 'born and lost in balance: a steady reactor' };
    if (k > 0.6)
      return { name: 'subcritical', colour: ACC, note: 'only the source keeps it lit' };
    return { name: 'subcritical', colour: DIM, note: 'the chain cannot sustain itself' };
  }

  /** A rough, clearly-labelled yield extrapolation for the fast regimes. */
  function projectedYield() {
    const k = kNow();
    if (k <= 1.02) return 0;
    // A reactor cannot produce a nuclear yield however far k climbs. Dilute fuel
    // means a slow chain, and the core boils and bursts apart long before much
    // of it burns. Chernobyl was a steam explosion, and this must not pretend
    // otherwise.
    if (state.enr < 0.2 || k < 1.3) return 0;
    // Efficiency is the fraction that fissions before the core blows itself
    // apart. Calibrated so the Hiroshima preset (64 kg at k = 2.05) gives 15 kt,
    // the real ~1.4% efficiency of a gun-type weapon.
    const eff = Math.max(0, Math.min(0.2, 0.01417 * (k - 1.0) ** 2));
    return coreMass() * state.enr * 17 * eff; // kilotons TNT
  }

  // --- canvas ----------------------------------------------------------------

  const canvas = document.getElementById('fis') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const { ctx, W, H } = setupCanvas(canvas, () => draw());
  const cssVar = (n: string) => getComputedStyle(canvas).getPropertyValue(n).trim();
  const inkRGB = () => cssVar('--stage-ink') || '232,233,236';
  const inkA = (a: number) => `rgba(${inkRGB()},${a})`;
  const { label, textWidth } = makeText(ctx, inkA);

  const NEUT = '#ffd24a';
  const FIRE = '#ff7a3c';
  const U235 = '#5b8cff';
  const LEAK = '#49b8ff';
  const CAPT = '#8a8f9c';
  const ACC = '#7c8cff';
  const GOOD = '#34d399';
  const WARN = '#f59e0b';
  const BAD = '#ef4444';
  const DIM = '#8a8f9c';

  // The core gets the whole top of the stage; the diagrams sit underneath it.
  const CORE = { x: 20, y: 84, w: 960, h: 616 };
  const POP = { x: 20, y: 736, w: 404, h: 284 };
  const KG = { x: 440, y: 736, w: 250, h: 284 };
  const YLD = { x: 706, y: 736, w: 274, h: 284 };

  function rr(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, r: number) {
    const rad = Math.min(r, Math.abs(w) / 2, Math.abs(h) / 2);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(x + rad, y);
    ctx.arcTo(x + w, y, x + w, y + h, rad);
    ctx.arcTo(x + w, y + h, x, y + h, rad);
    ctx.arcTo(x, y + h, x, y, rad);
    ctx.arcTo(x, y, x + w, y, rad);
    ctx.closePath();
  }
  function panel(p: { x: number; y: number; w: number; h: number }, title: string) {
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.1);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    rr(p.x, p.y, p.w, p.h, 10);
    ctx.stroke();
    label(title, p.x + 2, p.y - 9, 0.5, 'left', 11);
  }
  function dot(x: number, y: number, c: string, r = 4) {
    ctx.fillStyle = c;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(x, y, r, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
  }

  const coreC = () => ({ cx: CORE.x + CORE.w / 2, cy: CORE.y + CORE.h / 2 + 4 });
  const maxRpx = Math.min(CORE.w, CORE.h) / 2 - 40;
  // The core spans 2 cm to 90 cm, so the view zooms to keep it framed and a
  // scale bar carries the size. This shows leakage better than a growing disk:
  // what visibly changes is how far a neutron gets between collisions compared
  // with the core it is trying to escape.
  const ppc = () => maxRpx / (state.R * 1.12);
  const SX = (x: number) => coreC().cx + x * ppc();
  const SY = (y: number) => coreC().cy + y * ppc();

  function drawCore() {
    panel(CORE, 'THE CORE');
    const { cx, cy } = coreC();
    const Rpx = state.R * ppc();

    if (state.refl > 0.02) {
      ctx.fillStyle = `rgba(124,140,255,${0.05 + state.refl * 0.12})`;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(cx, cy, Rpx + 16, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.arc(cx, cy, Rpx, 0, Math.PI * 2, true);
      ctx.fill('evenodd');
      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(124,140,255,${0.25 + state.refl * 0.4})`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(cx, cy, Rpx + 16, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.stroke();
    }

    const hot = Math.min(1, state.enr / 0.9);
    const grd = ctx.createRadialGradient(cx, cy, Rpx * 0.1, cx, cy, Rpx);
    grd.addColorStop(0, `rgba(${90 + hot * 120},${110 - hot * 40},${180 - hot * 100},0.2)`);
    grd.addColorStop(1, `rgba(${90 + hot * 120},${110 - hot * 40},${180 - hot * 100},0.05)`);
    ctx.fillStyle = grd;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(cx, cy, Rpx, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.22);
    ctx.lineWidth = 2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(cx, cy, Rpx, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.stroke();

    // a fixed lattice of nuclei, coloured by isotope
    const nDots = 420;
    for (let i = 0; i < nDots; i++) {
      const a = (i * 2.399963) % (Math.PI * 2);
      const r2 = Math.sqrt((i + 0.5) / nDots) * Rpx * 0.97;
      const isU235 = ((i * 9301 + 49297) % 233280) / 233280 < state.enr;
      ctx.fillStyle = isU235 ? 'rgba(91,140,255,0.55)' : 'rgba(107,112,128,0.3)';
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(cx + Math.cos(a) * r2, cy + Math.sin(a) * r2, isU235 ? 2.3 : 1.7, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.fill();
    }

    // fission fragments recoiling apart
    for (const f of frags) {
      const a = Math.max(0, f.life);
      dot(SX(f.x), SY(f.y), `rgba(255,150,80,${a * 0.85})`, 2.6);
    }

    // the three fates, each with its own mark
    for (const e of events) {
      const a = Math.max(0, e.life);
      const x = SX(e.x), y = SY(e.y);
      if (e.kind === 'fission') {
        ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(255,122,60,${a})`;
        ctx.lineWidth = 2.4;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(x, y, 4 + (1 - a) * 20, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.stroke();
      } else if (e.kind === 'capture') {
        ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(138,143,156,${a * 0.85})`;
        ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
        const s = 5;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(x - s, y - s); ctx.lineTo(x + s, y + s);
        ctx.moveTo(x + s, y - s); ctx.lineTo(x - s, y + s);
        ctx.stroke();
      } else {
        const rad = Math.hypot(e.x, e.y) || 1;
        const nx = e.x / rad, ny = e.y / rad;
        ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(73,184,255,${a})`;
        ctx.lineWidth = 2;
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(x, y);
        ctx.lineTo(x + nx * (10 + (1 - a) * 22), y + ny * (10 + (1 - a) * 22));
        ctx.stroke();
      }
    }

    // free neutrons: a streak plus a head, with newborns haloed so each burst
    // of two or three reads as one fission handing on the chain
    const fresh = genTime() * 0.16;
    ctx.lineCap = 'round';
    for (const p of neutrons) {
      const x = SX(p.x), y = SY(p.y);
      ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(255,210,74,0.45)';
      ctx.lineWidth = 1.6;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(x - (p.vx / SPEED) * 9, y - (p.vy / SPEED) * 9);
      ctx.lineTo(x, y);
      ctx.stroke();
      if (p.age < fresh) {
        ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,236,150,0.28)';
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(x, y, 7, 0, Math.PI * 2);
        ctx.fill();
      }
      dot(x, y, NEUT, 2.4);
    }

    if (detonated) drawFireball(cx, cy, Rpx);

    if (!detonated && neutrons.length === 0 && !state.source) {
      label('the chain has died out', cx, cy - 8, 0.8, 'center', 15, DIM);
      label('press Reset, or raise k above one', cx, cy + 16, 0.5, 'center', 12);
    }

    // header
    label(`R = ${state.R.toFixed(1)} cm`, CORE.x + 8, CORE.y + 22, 0.85, 'left', 13, ACC);
    label(`${(state.enr * 100).toFixed(1)}% U-235`, CORE.x + 190, CORE.y + 22, 0.85, 'left', 13, U235);
    label(`generation ${Math.floor(gens)}`, CORE.x + CORE.w - 8, CORE.y + 22, 0.85, 'right', 13, WARN);

    // scale bar: the view is zoomed to fit, so this carries the size
    const nice = [1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100];
    const want = (state.R * 1.12) / 2.6;
    const barCm = nice.reduce((a, b) => (Math.abs(b - want) < Math.abs(a - want) ? b : a));
    const bx = CORE.x + 16, by = CORE.y + CORE.h - 44;
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.4);
    ctx.lineWidth = 1.5;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(bx, by - 5);
    ctx.lineTo(bx, by);
    ctx.lineTo(bx + barCm * ppc(), by);
    ctx.lineTo(bx + barCm * ppc(), by - 5);
    ctx.stroke();
    label(`${barCm} cm`, bx + (barCm * ppc()) / 2, by - 9, 0.5, 'center', 10);

    // legend
    const ly = CORE.y + CORE.h - 14;
    const leg: [string, string][] = [
      [NEUT, 'neutron'],
      [FIRE, 'fission'],
      [CAPT, 'captured'],
      [LEAK, 'escaped'],
      [U235, 'U-235'],
    ];
    let lx = CORE.x + 16;
    for (const [c, tx] of leg) {
      dot(lx + 4, ly - 4, c);
      label(tx, lx + 14, ly, 0.55, 'left', 11);
      lx += 22 + textWidth(tx, 11) + 20;
    }
  }

  /** Runs on the wall clock, so it keeps moving after physics has frozen. */
  function drawFireball(cx: number, cy: number, Rpx: number) {
    ctx.save();
    rr(CORE.x + 1, CORE.y + 1, CORE.w - 2, CORE.h - 2, 10);
    ctx.clip();
    const t = blastT;
    const grow = 1 - Math.exp(-t * 3.2);
    const rad = Rpx * (1 + grow * 5.5);
    const cool = Math.min(1, t / 2.2);
    const fb = ctx.createRadialGradient(cx, cy, 0, cx, cy, rad);
    fb.addColorStop(0, `rgba(255,255,${Math.round(240 - cool * 200)},${0.95 * (1 - cool * 0.75)})`);
    fb.addColorStop(0.45, `rgba(255,${Math.round(250 - cool * 160)},${Math.round(220 - cool * 200)},${0.8 * (1 - cool * 0.7)})`);
    fb.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(255,80,30,0)');
    ctx.fillStyle = fb;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.arc(cx, cy, rad, 0, Math.PI * 2);
    ctx.fill();
    if (t < 1.8) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = `rgba(255,236,200,${Math.max(0, 0.55 - t * 0.3)})`;
      ctx.lineWidth = 3;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.arc(cx, cy, Rpx + t * 700, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    ctx.restore();
    if (t > 0.35) {
      label('DETONATION', cx, cy - 12, 0.95, 'center', 30, '#fff');
      label(fmtYield(projectedYield()), cx, cy + 22, 0.95, 'center', 20, '#ffd9b0');
    }
  }

  function drawPop() {
    panel(POP, 'NEUTRON POPULATION (LOG)');
    const px = POP.x + 46, py = POP.y + 22, pw = POP.w - 62, ph = POP.h - 58;
    const top = Math.log(MAXN + 1);
    const Y = (n: number) => py + ph - (Math.log(n + 1) / top) * ph;

    ctx.lineWidth = 1;
    for (const g of [1, 10, 100, 1000]) {
      const y = Y(g);
      ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.14);
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(px, y);
      ctx.lineTo(px + pw, y);
      ctx.stroke();
      label(String(g), px - 6, y + 3, 0.4, 'right', 9);
    }
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.22);
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(px, py);
    ctx.lineTo(px, py + ph);
    ctx.lineTo(px + pw, py + ph);
    ctx.stroke();

    if (nHist.length > 1) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = regime().colour;
      ctx.lineWidth = 2;
      ctx.beginPath();
      nHist.forEach((n, i) => {
        const x = px + (i / (nHist.length - 1)) * pw;
        i ? ctx.lineTo(x, Y(n)) : ctx.moveTo(x, Y(n));
      });
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    label('time →', px + pw, py + ph + 16, 0.4, 'right', 10);
    label(`${neutrons.length} now`, px + 4, py + 12, 0.75, 'left', 12, NEUT);
    if (diverged) label('running away', px + pw, py + 12, 0.8, 'right', 11, BAD);
  }

  function drawK() {
    panel(KG, 'MULTIPLICATION k');
    const px = KG.x + 18, pw = KG.w - 36;
    const k = kNow();
    const r = regime();

    label(k.toFixed(hasMeasure ? 3 : 2), KG.x + KG.w / 2, KG.y + 66, 0.95, 'center', 42, r.colour);
    label(hasMeasure ? 'counted from the chain' : 'predicted for this setup',
      KG.x + KG.w / 2, KG.y + 88, 0.45, 'center', 10);

    const barY = KG.y + 118, barH = 18;
    ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.07);
    rr(px, barY, pw, barH, 4);
    ctx.fill();
    ctx.fillStyle = r.colour;
    rr(px, barY, Math.max(2, (Math.max(0, Math.min(2, k)) / 2) * pw), barH, 4);
    ctx.fill();
    const one = px + pw * 0.5;
    ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.55);
    ctx.lineWidth = 2;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(one, barY - 6);
    ctx.lineTo(one, barY + barH + 6);
    ctx.stroke();
    label('k = 1', one, barY - 10, 0.55, 'center', 10);
    label('0', px, barY + barH + 16, 0.4, 'left', 10);
    label('2', px + pw, barY + barH + 16, 0.4, 'right', 10);

    label(r.name.toUpperCase(), KG.x + KG.w / 2, barY + 52, 0.95, 'center', 14, r.colour);
    label(`k∞ = ${kInfinite().toFixed(2)} with no leakage`, KG.x + KG.w / 2, barY + 76, 0.45, 'center', 10);
    label(`${fissions.toLocaleString()} fissions`, KG.x + KG.w / 2, barY + 96, 0.5, 'center', 10, WARN);
  }

  function drawYield() {
    panel(YLD, 'WHERE THIS LANDS');
    const px = YLD.x + 16;
    const lw = YLD.w - 32;
    const r = regime();
    let y = YLD.y + 30;

    label(r.name.toUpperCase(), px, y, 0.95, 'left', 14, r.colour);
    y += 19;
    label(r.note, px, y, 0.5, 'left', 10);
    y += 24;
    label(`${fissions.toLocaleString()} fissions · ${eng(fissions * E_FISSION, 'J', 1)}`, px, y, 0.5, 'left', 10, WARN);
    y += 22;

    const ky = projectedYield();
    const lo = 0.0002, hi = 600;
    const LX = (v: number) => px + ((Math.log(Math.max(v, lo)) - Math.log(lo)) / (Math.log(hi) - Math.log(lo))) * lw;
    const barY = y + 4;
    ctx.fillStyle = inkA(0.07);
    rr(px, barY, lw, 12, 4);
    ctx.fill();
    if (ky > 0) {
      const g = ctx.createLinearGradient(px, 0, px + lw, 0);
      g.addColorStop(0, WARN);
      g.addColorStop(1, BAD);
      ctx.fillStyle = g;
      rr(px, barY, Math.max(3, LX(ky) - px), 12, 4);
      ctx.fill();
    }
    for (const v of [0.0005, 0.5, 15, 21, 300]) {
      ctx.strokeStyle = inkA(0.28);
      ctx.lineWidth = 1;
      ctx.beginPath();
      ctx.moveTo(LX(v), barY - 3);
      ctx.lineTo(LX(v), barY + 15);
      ctx.stroke();
    }
    label('grenade', LX(0.0005), barY + 28, 0.4, 'center', 9);
    label('Hiroshima', LX(15), barY + 28, 0.5, 'center', 9, BAD);
    label('warhead', LX(300), barY + 28, 0.4, 'center', 9);

    y = barY + 52;
    if (ky > 0) {
      label(`≈ ${fmtYield(ky)}`, px, y, 0.95, 'left', 18, BAD);
      label('rough order of magnitude', px, y + 18, 0.4, 'left', 9);
    } else if (kNow() > 1.02 && state.enr < 0.2) {
      label('an excursion, not a yield', px, y, 0.8, 'left', 12, WARN);
      label('reactor fuel is too dilute to detonate:', px, y + 17, 0.45, 'left', 9);
      label('it boils and bursts apart first, which', px, y + 30, 0.45, 'left', 9);
      label('is exactly what Chernobyl did.', px, y + 43, 0.45, 'left', 9);
    } else if (kNow() > 1.02) {
      label('would fizzle', px, y, 0.75, 'left', 12, WARN);
      label('too slow to burn before it scatters', px, y + 17, 0.45, 'left', 9);
    } else {
      label('no explosive yield', px, y, 0.6, 'left', 12, GOOD);
      label('the chain does not run away', px, y + 17, 0.45, 'left', 9);
    }
  }

  function fmtYield(kt: number): string {
    if (kt >= 1) return `${kt.toFixed(kt < 10 ? 1 : 0)} kt TNT`;
    return `${(kt * 1000).toFixed(kt * 1000 < 10 ? 1 : 0)} t TNT`;
  }
  function eng(x: number, unit: string, digits = 2): string {
    const a = Math.abs(x);
    if (a === 0) return `0 ${unit}`;
    const steps: [number, string][] = [
      [1e-12, 'p'], [1e-9, 'n'], [1e-6, 'µ'], [1e-3, 'm'],
      [1, ''], [1e3, 'k'], [1e6, 'M'], [1e9, 'G'],
    ];
    let pick = steps[0];
    for (const s of steps) if (a >= s[0]) pick = s;
    return `${(x / pick[0]).toFixed(digits)} ${pick[1]}${unit}`;
  }

  // --- readouts --------------------------------------------------------------

  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;
  const setRow = (n: number, lab: string, val: string, col?: string) => {
    el('l' + n).innerHTML = subHtml(lab);
    const b = el('r' + n);
    b.textContent = val;
    if (col) b.style.color = col;
    else b.style.removeProperty('color');
  };
  const setEq = equationBar();
  function updateReadout() {
    const r = regime();
    const m = coreMass();
    setRow(1, 'Multiplication k', kNow().toFixed(hasMeasure ? 3 : 2), r.colour);
    setRow(2, 'Generation', String(Math.floor(gens)));
    setRow(3, 'Free neutrons', neutrons.length.toLocaleString());
    setRow(4, 'Fissions', fissions.toLocaleString());
    setRow(5, 'Core mass (sphere)', m < 1000 ? `${m.toFixed(m < 10 ? 1 : 0)} kg` : `${(m / 1000).toFixed(1)} t`);
    setRow(6, 'State', r.name, r.colour);
    setEq([
      [
        ['k', r.colour],
        '=\\dfrac{\\text{neutrons in the next generation}}{\\text{neutrons in this one}}',
        '\\quad N_g = N_0\\,k^{\\,g}',
      ],
      [
        [`k = ${kNow().toFixed(hasMeasure ? 3 : 2)}`, r.colour],
        `\\quad g = ${Math.floor(gens)}`,
        `\\quad N = ${neutrons.length}`,
        [`\\quad\\text{${r.name}}`, r.colour],
      ],
      [
        'k just under one dies away, k just over one runs away: the whole of reactor control is that one digit',
      ],
    ]);
  }

  // --- loop ------------------------------------------------------------------

  function draw() {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, W, H);
    label('Nuclear Fission', 20, 30, 0.85, 'left', 15);
    label('every neutron either splits a nucleus, is swallowed, or leaks away', 20, 54, 0.5, 'left', 11, GOOD);
    drawCore();
    drawPop();
    drawK();
    drawYield();
  }

  let last = performance.now();
  let sampleClock = 0;
  function frame(now: number) {
    let dtReal = (now - last) / 1000;
    last = now;
    dtReal = Math.min(0.05, Math.max(0, dtReal));

    if (state.running && !detonated) {
      // a neutron covers one core radius every CROSS_SECONDS / rate seconds
      const s = sigmas(state.enr, state.rod);
      const advance = ((state.R / CROSS_SECONDS) * state.rate * dtReal) / SPEED;
      // keep each sub-step short next to the mean free path and the core, so no
      // neutron ever steps clean over the boundary
      const dsMax = Math.min(0.4 / s.St, 0.05 * state.R);
      const steps = Math.min(80, Math.max(1, Math.ceil(advance / (dsMax / SPEED))));
      const dt = advance / steps;
      for (let i = 0; i < steps; i++) step(dt);
    }

    // marks and the fireball run on the wall clock, so they look the same in
    // every scenario and keep moving once the physics has frozen
    for (let i = events.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      events[i].life -= dtReal * 1.6;
      if (events[i].life <= 0) events.splice(i, 1);
    }
    for (let i = frags.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      frags[i].x += frags[i].vx * dtReal;
      frags[i].y += frags[i].vy * dtReal;
      frags[i].life -= dtReal * 1.8;
      if (frags[i].life <= 0) frags.splice(i, 1);
    }
    if (detonated) blastT += dtReal;

    sampleClock += dtReal;
    if (sampleClock > 0.05) {
      sampleClock = 0;
      nHist.push(neutrons.length);
      if (nHist.length > 380) nHist.shift();
    }
    draw();
    updateReadout();
    requestAnimationFrame(frame);
  }

  // --- controls --------------------------------------------------------------

  const runBtn = el('run');
  runBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    if (detonated) build();
    state.running = !state.running;
    runBtn.textContent = state.running ? 'Pause' : 'Run';
  });
  el('reset').addEventListener('click', () => {
    build();
    state.running = true;
    runBtn.textContent = 'Pause';
  });

  function setSlider(id: string, value: number) {
    const inp = el(id) as HTMLInputElement;
    inp.value = String(value);
    inp.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
  }
  function bindRange(id: string, apply: (n: number) => void) {
    const inp = el(id) as HTMLInputElement;
    inp.addEventListener('input', () => apply(+inp.value));
    apply(+inp.value);
  }

  // The geometry and material knobs re-run the quick Monte Carlo, so k answers
  // the slider even with no chain running.
  bindRange('enr', (n) => {
    state.enr = n / 100;
    el('enrval').textContent = `${n.toFixed(1)}%`;
    refreshK();
  });
  bindRange('size', (n) => {
    state.R = n / 10;
    el('szval').textContent = `${state.R.toFixed(1)} cm`;
    refreshK();
  });
  bindRange('refl', (n) => {
    state.refl = n / 100;
    el('refval').textContent = `${n}%`;
    refreshK();
  });
  bindRange('rod', (n) => {
    state.rod = n / 100;
    el('rodval').textContent = `${n}%`;
    refreshK();
  });
  // log-scaled so slow motion gets real resolution: 0.02× to 20×
  const RATE_MIN = 0.02, RATE_DEC = 3;
  const rateSlider = (r: number) => Math.round((Math.log10(r / RATE_MIN) / RATE_DEC) * 100);
  bindRange('rate', (v) => {
    state.rate = RATE_MIN * Math.pow(10, (v / 100) * RATE_DEC);
    el('rateval').textContent =
      state.rate < 1 ? `${state.rate.toFixed(2)}×` : `${state.rate.toFixed(1)}×`;
  });
  (el('autoctl') as HTMLInputElement).addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    state.auto = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).checked;
  });
  (el('src') as HTMLInputElement).addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    state.source = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).checked;
  });

  // Verified by Monte Carlo: k = 0.03, 1.02, 1.09 and 2.05 respectively. The
  // bomb preset is 64 kg of 90% U-235, which is Little Boy's actual load.
  // Each scenario also picks its own pace: slow enough to follow single
  // neutrons where that is the point, fast enough to watch a chain evolve
  // where that is.
  const SCENES: Record<
    Scenario,
    { enr: number; size: number; refl: number; rod: number; auto: boolean; src: boolean; rate: number }
  > = {
    lump: { enr: 0.7, size: 156, refl: 0, rod: 0, auto: false, src: true, rate: 0.3 },
    reactor: { enr: 6, size: 692, refl: 70, rod: 35, auto: true, src: true, rate: 8 },
    chernobyl: { enr: 6, size: 692, refl: 70, rod: 15, auto: false, src: true, rate: 8 },
    bomb: { enr: 90, size: 93, refl: 85, rod: 0, auto: false, src: false, rate: 4 },
  };

  const BRIEF: Record<Scenario, { title: string; body: string }> = {
    lump: {
      title: 'A lump of natural uranium',
      body:
        'Uranium exactly as it comes out of the ground: 0.7% U-235 and 99.3% U-238, which swallows neutrons without splitting. k never gets near one, so the chain dies as fast as the background source feeds it. Turn the time rate right down and follow a single neutron to its end. This is why uranium ore is safe to handle, and why enrichment, not uranium, is the hard part of building anything nuclear.',
    },
    reactor: {
      title: 'A working power reactor',
      body:
        'The rods sit about a third of the way in and a controller trims them continuously so that k stays pinned at one: every fission replaces itself exactly once, no more. Held for years on end, that balance is electricity. The population graph is flat not because nothing is happening, but because births and losses are matched to three decimal places.',
    },
    chernobyl: {
      title: 'Reactor 4, Chernobyl · 26 April 1986, 01:23',
      body:
        'An RBMK-1000 running a turbine coast-down test. Power had been allowed to collapse to roughly 200 MW thermal, far under the planned figure, and xenon build-up left it sluggish to raise. To claw it back the operators withdrew nearly every control rod, leaving far fewer inserted than the safety margin required. The RBMK had a positive void coefficient: when its cooling water boiled, reactivity rose instead of falling. With the rods out there was nothing left to absorb the surge, and power climbed roughly a hundredfold in about four seconds. The rods finally slammed in had graphite tips that briefly added reactivity rather than killing it. What followed was a steam explosion and a graphite fire, not a nuclear detonation. This is that core, with the rods left out and k sitting just above one.',
    },
    bomb: {
      title: 'Little Boy, Hiroshima · 6 August 1945',
      body:
        'About 64 kg of uranium enriched to roughly 80% U-235, fired together inside a tamper by a gun barrel to form one supercritical mass. k jumps to about two, so the chain doubles every generation and the entire assembly reacts in under a millionth of a second. Only about 1.4% of the uranium actually fissioned, and that was enough for 15 kilotons.',
    },
  };

  function applyScene(s: Scenario) {
    state.scenario = s;
    const c = SCENES[s];
    (el('autoctl') as HTMLInputElement).checked = c.auto;
    state.auto = c.auto;
    (el('src') as HTMLInputElement).checked = c.src;
    state.source = c.src;
    setSlider('enr', c.enr);
    setSlider('size', c.size);
    setSlider('refl', c.refl);
    setSlider('rod', c.rod);
    setSlider('rate', rateSlider(c.rate));
    el('brief-title').textContent = BRIEF[s].title;
    el('brief-body').textContent = BRIEF[s].body;
    build();
    state.running = true;
    runBtn.textContent = 'Pause';
  }

  el('scenario').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('button');
    if (!btn) return;
    document.querySelectorAll('#scenario button').forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('active'));
    btn.classList.add('active');
    applyScene(btn.dataset.s as Scenario);
  });

  applyScene('lump');
  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
</script>

<style>
  .fis-canvas {
    touch-action: none;
  }
  .scale-hint {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    margin-top: 3px;
  }
  .check {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .check input {
    accent-color: var(--accent);
